WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
When you came full circle and like the pandemic started

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.840
and you have two young children.

3
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.720
Yes.
What was that like?

4
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.330
Yeah, it was a rough time,

5
00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.650
and it was why I wrote the book.

6
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.100 line:15%
We were sitting around with the pandemic,

7
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.430 line:15%
and George Floyd had been murdered,

8
00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:23.030 line:15%
and there was anti-Asian violence erupting,

9
00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:24.740 line:15%
and the kids were under foot

10
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:27.630
and we were just sort of searching for a way

11
00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.440
to explain it all to them.

12
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.150
And as a result, I started thinking about another incident

13
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.393
of police brutality when I was younger,

14
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.960
the Rodney King beating.

15
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.730
And so I started thinking about the immigrant journey also,

16
00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.250
like what is this country that I thought I was coming to,

17
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:48.370
and what is it now, and all this.

18
00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:52.423
And so the book is about a boy,

19
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.530
a bit of a troublemaker, who comes home, he's suspended.

20
00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:00.643
And his parents own a liquor store in South Central L.A.

21
00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.480
And it's April 29th, 1992,

22
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.200
the day the verdict is being read.

23
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.720
The father goes to the store to board it up.

24
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.680
And the boy, seeing the news reports,

25
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.210
grows increasingly desperate

26
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:16.730
and decides that the best way to protect his father

27
00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.160
is to take his dad's gun from the closet

28
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.793
and deliver it to him for protection that night.

29
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:23.870
So it's a poor decision,

30
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.960
but an interesting story as the boy

31
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.320
tries to get across Los Angeles.

32
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.520
And is it true that your sons read it?

33
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.660
Yes, yeah.
And like contributed

34
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.603
to like compliments?

35
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.900
Or did you run it by them?

36
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.700
Yeah, he was my authenticity reader at the end.

37
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.940
I was like, "How'd I do, how'd I do?"

38
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.477
And he said, "Good job, Daddy."

39
00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:51.500
What did your parents like think?

40
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.480
I mean, you moved here from Korea at a young age.

41
00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.720
Did they instill like their honest life to you

42
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.700
and life lessons about your future?

43
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.020
What did they think when you told them

44
00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.663
you wanted to be an actor?

45
00:02:03.663 --> 00:02:05.560
When I told them I wanted to be an actor,

46
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:06.910
I think they were just really worried

47
00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.660 line:15%
that I was going to be found dead

48
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.940 line:15%
in an apartment from starvation, you know?

49
00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.120 line:15%
And they were just worried

50
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.640 line:15%
that I wouldn't be able to pay the bills.

51
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.535
My dad did say, "You wanna become an actor?"

52
00:02:20.535 --> 00:02:23.490
And this is the '90s, so I think all he saw

53
00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.340
was Connie Chung on television.

54
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.010
And he said, "Maybe you wanna read the news?

55
00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.990
Is that somewhere you wanna go?"

56
00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.480
And I said, "That's not what I wanna do."

57
00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.203
But I can see the logic.

58
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.683
That was an Asian lady.

59
00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.690
And I think he just didn't feel

60
00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.270
that there were any opportunities for Asians on television.

61
00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:44.100
Did you ever get their stamp of approval?

62
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.930
I did, because when I was in the,

63
00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.340
there was a small feature in the local Korean newspaper.

64
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.710
That's when I had made it in their eyes.

65
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.410
See, isn't that beautiful?
Yeah.

66
00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.570
Well, speaking of actors and films and movies,

67
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.730
we were just chatting all things Oscars.

68
00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:06.900
Do you have any favorites this year?

69
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.630
The one that stood out to me,

70
00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:12.090
and I still have to do my viewing, is "King Richard."

71
00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:16.000
And I think Will Smith has never been better.

72
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.270
I just have to say, as one human to another,

73
00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.480
I really am like one of your biggest fans

74
00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.726
and I'm so excited that you've come on the show!

75
00:03:22.726 --> 00:03:25.857
(audience clapping)
I love you!

76
00:03:25.857 --> 00:03:28.223
John Cho, get your copy of "Troublemaker."

77
00:03:28.223 --> 00:03:30.097
It's in stores now!

78
00:03:30.097 --> 00:03:32.680 line:15%
(upbeat music)

